\begin{abstract}
Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) for historical law must retrieve connected authorities without treating overruled doctrine as current law. We introduce TERRA, an experimental GraphRAG pipeline combining an Event Evolution Graph over \CorpusTotalOpinions\ Supreme Court opinions, generic five-step procedural scaffolds, EASY/HARD routing and LLM-prompted evidence checking. Its scaffolds are not case-specific judicial reasoning. A historical \numUniqueQueries-query, five-system evaluation uses two non-Llama judges and paired tests with Benjamini--Hochberg correction. TERRA's Category~B faithfulness exceeds the retrieval baselines but remains below Direct LLM; its overall and answer-only scores describe different refusal and answered subsets. Adversarial safe handling is \TerraCatDSafeHandlingRate, with \TerraCatDFatalErrors\ fatal errors and false refusals on \TerraCatAFalseRefusals\ Category~A and \TerraCatBFalseRefusals\ Category~B queries. The obscure-precedent advantage depends on curated overruling annotations. A separate prospective comparison evaluates graph retrieval (T) against dense--BM25 retrieval (H) on the same 350 queries with shared evidence budgets, verification, generation and judges. On 100 Category~B pairs, mean evidence faithfulness is 0.76400 for T and 0.49515 for H (paired difference 0.26885; bootstrap 95\% CI [0.16035, 0.37205]; randomization $p\approx0.000010$). T triggers 69/100 verifier refusals versus H's 4/100: the gain concerns the gated pipeline, not demonstrated answer coverage or legal correctness. External and temporal evaluations remain mixed. Historical configuration gaps, annotation dependence and absent licensed-practitioner validation restrict the findings to an experimental research framework.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
\textcolor{black}{\textit{Retrieval-Augmented Generation, Large Language Models, Temporal Knowledge Graph, Agentic Reasoning, Semantic Entailment, Explainable AI, Temporal Leakage Evaluation}}
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:Introduction}
Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) decouples the parametric memory of Large Language Models (LLMs) from external knowledge bases, enabling factual grounding and source attribution without continuous retraining. In high-stakes settings, where errors and hallucinations carry real consequences, the fidelity of the retrieved context is a strict operational requirement, and naive ``retrieve-then-generate'' pipelines over static vector databases and fixed-length chunks can struggle with conflicting authorities and multi-step evidence.

\subsection{Summary of Contributions}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Dual-Index Architecture:} a judicial GraphRAG corpus cross-linking an EEG of \CorpusTotalOpinions\ SCOTUS decisions (\CorpusPrecedentEdges\ citation edges) with a node-level generic procedural scaffold (one five-step template per case, with no case-specific issue, test or holding) and no synthetic cases.
    \item \textbf{Traffic Cop Routing with a Coarse Domain Flag:} an SLM-driven complexity router with a domain-relevance flag derived from a title match or nearest-neighbour proximity ($d_{L2}<1.3$ in the current source). This flag is not itself an early-refusal rule; the canonical evaluation terminated no query before retrieval (Sections~\ref{subsec:computational-budget} and~\ref{subsec:firewall-analysis}).
    \item \textbf{NLI-Based Entailment Evaluation and XAI Attribution:} an LLM-prompted entailment grader that evaluates retrieved evidence and gates generation, with generic procedural scaffolds and citation provenance; its interception reliability is unvalidated; a pilot ablation included five unique adversarial queries.
    \item \textbf{Leakage Safeguards and Audits:} safeguards against temporal precedent inversion, benchmark retrieval contamination and cross-domain feature bleeding, with mixed results in the leakage probe.
    \item \textbf{Historical Empirical Evaluation:} across \numTotalEvals\ evaluations (\numUniqueQueries\ queries $\times$ \numComparativePipelines\ pipelines, $T=0.0$) with paired Wilcoxon tests, Cohen's $d_z$ and 95\% bootstrap CIs, TERRA has the highest Answer-Only Faithfulness among RAG architectures ($\TerraAnswerOnlyFaithfulness$ vs.\ HippoRAG's $\HippoRAGAnswerOnlyFaithfulness$), outperforms Flat Vector RAG ($\Delta = \CatBTerraVsFlatDelta$, $p = \CatBTerraVsFlatWilcoxonP$, $d_z = \CatBTerraVsFlatDz$) and HippoRAG ($\Delta = \CatBTerraVsHippoRAGDelta$, $p = \CatBTerraVsHippoRAGWilcoxonP$, $d_z = \CatBTerraVsHippoRAGDz$) on Category~B, delivers $\TerraCatDSafeHandlingRate$ adversarial safe handling ($\TerraCatDSafeHandling$: \TerraCatDRejection\ refusals, \TerraCatDRefutations\ refutations) with \TerraCatDFatalErrors\ fatal errors vs.\ $\BaselineFatalMin$--$\BaselineFatalMax$ for baselines, and incurs \TerraCatAFalseRefusals\ false refusals on Category~A (\TerraCatBFalseRefusals\ on B). Direct LLM scores higher on Category~B ($\Delta=\CatBTerraVsDirectLLMDelta$), and the Flat margin's bootstrap CI includes zero. Evaluations rely on automated LLM judges without licensed attorneys, making human review an essential prerequisite (Section~\ref{subsec:limitations}).
\end{itemize}

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related-work}
Retrieval-Augmented Generation \cite{lewis2020retrieval, guu2020realm} decoupled parametric memory from external knowledge, but standard RAG struggles with knowledge drift and multi-step reasoning. Graph-based architectures structure retrieved evidence: GraphRAG \cite{edge2024graphrag} builds hierarchical entity-relation communities for global summarization, and Think-on-Graph \cite{sun2024thinkongraph} runs iterative LLM-guided reasoning over knowledge graphs. In temporal domains, Liu and Cao \cite{liu2026raglearningunderstandinglimits} introduced a continuous-knowledge-drift benchmark and Chronos, a time-aware method organizing evidence into an Event Evolution Graph (EEG); Zhang et al. \cite{zhang2025respectingtemporalcausalconsistencyentityevent} proposed E2RAG for temporal-causal consistency across entity-event subgraphs; and Zhu et al. \cite{zhu2025rightanswerrighttime} developed STAR-RAG for time-sensitive rule graph summarization. In law, Chen et al.~\cite{chen2026legalgraphrag} proposed LegalGraphRAG, a Researcher, Auditor and Adjudicator multi-agent system over a hierarchical legal graph, and de Martim~\cite{demartim2025satgraphrag} introduced SAT-Graph RAG to trace diachronic statutory norm versions across constitutional amendments. These systems organize sources by abstraction level or model formal amendment events, whereas common-law systems also require tracking how precedent evolves across citation networks, where earlier decisions remain presumptive authority unless distinguished, limited or overruled; and motivates testing graph-grounded retrieval with explicit evidence checking on historical precedent questions.

